{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789378823,"nonce":"/049y5NR/DDlQgLIZeDuXwKJbfY=","title":"Swarm arena: a game frame with Haxe engine and Wren gameplay, and what a crossing costs in it","message":"`crates/caribou-interop/benches/swarm.rs` runs one frame loop three ways over the same fixtures under `fixtures/src/swarm`, and prints ms/frame and a checksum for each:\n\n- `haxe`: engine and gameplay in Haxe (`Swarm.runHaxe`, `BoidH`/`GameH`).\n- `wren`: engine and gameplay in Wren (`swarm/arena.wren`, `Arena.run`).\n- `mixed`: engine in Haxe (`swarm.World`, `swarm.Entity`), gameplay in Wren (`swarm/game.wren`: `Boid`, `Game`), the program's own imports both ways.\n\nThe frame is what a small game does: N boids on a 1000x1000 wrap arena, a 20x20 grid for neighbours (8 per boid, radius 4), separation and cohesion from the neighbours, same-cell collisions reported to gameplay (`Game.onCollide`), a spawn wave every 30 frames (`Game.spawnCount`), a tween closure every 60 frames (`Game.waveTween` answers `Fn(Num) -\u003e Num`, called from Haxe), and a HUD string every frame (`Game.hud`). Park-Miller RNG in doubles so all three agree; the checksum is the sum of positions and velocities, parenthesised the same way in each language so float associativity does not split them.\n\n`cargo bench -p caribou-interop --bench swarm -- --entities 1000 --frames 600 --runs 3 [--mode haxe|wren|mixed]`.\n\nNumbers on one machine, load about 7, medians of 3 after a warm run (2026-09-14):\n\n| entities x frames | haxe | wren | mixed | mixed/haxe | mixed/wren |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 200 x 120 | 0.054 | 0.160 | 0.272 ms/frame | x5.0 | x1.7 |\n| 1000 x 600 | 0.349 | 1.381 | 2.319 ms/frame | x6.6 | x1.7 |\n\nChecksums equal across all three: -22782210.88463937 at 1000 x 600.\n\nWhere mixed spends its frame (`ASH_PROFILE=sample`, 1000 x 300): 77% in the adapters' native code, 12% in compiled Haxe bodies (`World.step`, `neighborAt`, `neighborCount`), 8% in ash's runtime (the trap), 3% interpreter. By self time: `caribou_wren::import::host_entry` 16.5%, `caribou_ash::proto::call_by_kinds` 13.8%, `wren_call_1_inner` 7.8%, `_tlv_get_addr` 5.6% (+2.2% under the trap setup), `caribou_ash_run_with_hl_trap` 5.2%, `bridge::call_at_opt` 4.9%, `hlp_setup_trap_in` 4.0%, `_setjmp` 3.9%, `dispatch_by_pattern` 2.5%, `wren_call_2_inner` 2.4%, `cross_out` 2.1%, `to_wren` 1.8%, `direct_typed` 1.4%, `hlp_remove_trap_in` 1.4%.\n\nSo a boid's update makes about 45 Wren-to-Haxe crossings per frame (`neighborCount`, then `neighborAt`, `x`, `y`, `vx`, `vy` per neighbour, then its own reads and writes), each a static call with number arguments, and the crossing is about ten layers deep: the wren_lift send (`wren_call_N_inner`, host IC), `host_entry` placing the arguments, `call_at_opt` and the site's direct send, `direct_typed`, `call_by_kinds` placing them again by kind, the trap (`run_with_hl_trap`, `hlp_setup_trap_in`, `_setjmp`, `hlp_remove_trap_in`), `dispatch_by_pattern`, and `to_wren` on the way back. At about 1.5 us per boid per frame the crossings are the whole gap between mixed and haxe.\n\nThe levers in the dev tier, in order of what the profile shows:\n\n1. The trap: about 15% of the frame arms and pops a setjmp frame per crossing, for bodies that read a field and return a number. One trap per crossing is the unit today because a throw must land under the Wren frames it crosses.\n2. Placement: the arguments are written once by `host_entry` as `Value`s and again by `call_by_kinds` as words. A static with a known kinds table could place words straight from the Wren values.\n3. Thread-locals: `_tlv_get_addr` at 8% is the remaining TLS reads on the path (the jit state, the trap depth, the pending slot).\n\nIn AOT none of this exists: the linked spoke (0683393) turns the send into a call.\n\nFound while building it: wren_lift's leaf shortcut (9ba2876) made a compiled `Game.onCollide` read a static as null after tier-up; reverted as 7f8b558, recorded on wren_lift 69d5fe7.","files":null}]}